End-to-End AWS CI/CD Pipeline
using:

  GitHub
  CodePipeline
  CodeBuild
  S3
  CodeDeploy
  IAM
  EC2
  Node.js

This is REAL DevOps workflow.

Step1: Create IAM roles 

1.1 EC2 instance role (for CodeDeploy + S3)
Go to IAM → Roles → Create role

Trusted entity: AWS service

Use case: EC2

Attach policies:

AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess

AWSCodeDeployFullAccess 

Name: EC2-CodeDeploy-Role

Create role

Explanation
 This role allows the EC2 instance to download deployment files from S3 and communicate with CodeDeploy.

1.2 CodeDeploy service role
IAM → Roles → Create role

Trusted entity: AWS service

Use case: CodeDeploy

Attach policy:

AWSCodeDeployRole

Name: CodeDeploy-Service-Role

Create role
Explanation
This allows CodeDeploy to manage and coordinate deployments.

1.3 CodeBuild service role
IAM → Roles → Create role

Trusted entity: AWS service

Use case: CodeBuild

Attach:

AWSCodeBuildDeveloperAccess

AmazonS3FullAccess 

Name: CodeBuild-Service-Role

Create role
Explanation
Allows CodeBuild to compile the app and store build output artifacts in S3.

Step:2. Create EC2 instance for deployment 
Code deploy deploys the application in this instance

       2.1 Go to EC2 → Instances → Launch instances


Name: nodejs-app-ec2

Give tag : key = Name , value = CodeDeployTarget


AMI: Amazon Linux 2


Type: t2.micro


Key pair: any (for emergency access, but you won’t use SSH)


Network settings:


Security group:


Allow HTTP (80) from 0.0.0.0/0


IAM instance profile: select EC2-CodeDeploy-Role

2.2 Now connect to ec2 instance and install code deploy agent

1. sudo su
2. yum update -y
3. yum install -y ruby wget
4. wget https://aws-codedeploy-ap-south-1.s3.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/latest/install
5. chmod +x ./install
6. ./install auto
7. systemctl start codedeploy-agent
8. systemctl enable codedeploy-agent


Step :3. CodeDeploy application & deployment group
3.1 Application
Go to CodeDeploy → Applications → Create application


Name: Nodejs-app


Compute platform: EC2/On-premises


Create



3.2 Deployment group
Open Nodejs-app → Create deployment group


Name: NodejsApp-DeploymentGroup


Service role: CodeDeploy-Service-Role


Environment:


Amazon EC2 instances


Tag:


Key: Name


Value: CodeDeployTarget


Deployment configuration: CodeDeployDefault.AllAtOnce


Load balancer: unchecked (leave it if needed)


Create deployment group

Step:4. CodeBuild project (Node.js build)
4.1 Create project
Go to CodeBuild → Build projects → Create build project


name: Nodejs-app-build


4.2 Source
Source provider: GitHub


For creation, just link to your repo (connect with your github); CodePipeline will actually provide the source later.	
4.3 Environment
Environment image: Managed image


OS: Amazon Linux 2


Runtime: Standard


Image: default latest


Environment type: Linux


Service role: select CodeBuild-Service-Role


Allow CodeBuild to modify this role: Yes


4.4 Buildspec
Use a buildspec file
 (so it uses buildspec.yml from repo root)


4.5 Artifacts
Because in your UI you only see No artifacts and S3:
Choose: No artifacts
CodePipeline will handle artifacts itself.
4.6 Create project
Click Create build project.
 	Don’t start any build manually code pipeline handles it.

Step:5. CodePipeline (GitHub → CodeBuild → CodeDeploy)
5.1 Start pipeline creation 
Go to CodePipeline → Pipelines → Create pipeline


On the first screen (Templates), choose:


Category: Build custom pipeline


Template: Build custom pipeline


Click Next



5.2 Step 2 – Pipeline settings
Pipeline name: Nodejs-Full-Pipeline


Service role:


Use existing: CodePipeline-Service-Role


Or let it create a new one


Artifact store: default S3 (ok)


Click Next



5.3 Step 3 – Source (GitHub via CodeConnections)
Source provider: GitHub (via GitHub App)


Connection:


Choose your existing CodeConnections ARN
 (or Connect to GitHub, authorize, then come back)


Repository name:


Ensure it is set to Yaswanthy23/CICD-working


If “Not Found” shows, type full path manually:
 Yaswanthy23/CICD-working


Default branch: main


Output artifact format: CodePipeline default


Optional: turn on Webhook (trigger on push)


Click Next



5.4 Step 4 – Build stage 
		Sometimes it not works properly so skip it for now , we can add it later

5.5 Step 5 – Deploy stage
Deploy provider: AWS CodeDeploy


Region: ap-south-1


Application name: Nodejs-app


Deployment group: NodejsApp-DeploymentGroup


Click Next
5.6 Step 6 & 7 – Review & Create
Review settings


Click Create pipeline

Step:6. Add Build stage to existing pipeline
Now we insert CodeBuild in the middle.
Open CodePipeline → Nodejs-Full-Pipeline


Click Edit (top-right)


6.1 Add Build stage
Click + Add stage between Source and Deploy


Stage name: Build


Click Add stage


Inside the new Build stage:
Click + Add action group


Fill:
Action name: CodeBuildAction


Action provider: AWS CodeBuild


Region: ap-south-1


Input artifacts:


Select SourceArtifact (or the source artifact name created by the Source stage)


Project name:


Choose Nodejs-app-build from dropdown


Output artifacts:


Type: BuildArtifact


Click Done.
6.2 Save pipeline
Click Save


Confirm edits
Step:7. Run the pipeline
To run the pipeline ,Click Release change (top-right)
- New s3 bucket is created, Artifile is stored in this bucket
- To access application , copy the instance public IP and paste it on browser.








